You've heard about it. Maybe a friend sent you a video of someone sprinting through a laser maze, or you caught a clip of a group absolutely losing it during a VR zombie shooter. Now you're curious: what actually happens when you walk through the doors?

Here's the full picture — from arrival to the last sip of bubble tea.

## Arriving at 546 Yates St

Right in the heart of Downtown Victoria. No car, no problem. Walk over from your hotel, your office, or wherever the day has taken you. That accessibility is deliberate, and it's part of why Infusion Play has kept a loyal following in Victoria since 2016.

Walk in without a reservation, or lock in your session ahead of time at infusion-play.com. Both work. The walk-in option is especially handy for spontaneous evenings, rainy afternoons, or tourist days when plans shift without warning.

## Three Genuinely Different Things to Do Here

Infusion Play isn't one activity with a fancy name slapped on it. It's three distinct experience formats under one roof, and you can mix them however you like.

### 1. The VR Arena: Free-Roaming, Rifle in Hand

This is not sit-down VR. You move. You run. You dodge.

The VR Arena runs full free-roaming sessions with titles like After the Fall and Arizona Sunshine — both multiplayer zombie shooters where you and your group move through real physical space while the headset drops you somewhere else entirely. Rifle-style controllers make it feel physical in a way standard VR setups simply don't.

If you've only tried stationary VR before, this is a different thing. Your legs get involved. Your heart rate climbs. You'll be genuinely tired by the end, and you'll want to go again immediately.

### 2. Interactive Play Rooms: Body and Brain Together

The physical challenge rooms are where Infusion Play earns its reputation for variety. The laser maze alone is worth the trip. Duck, jump, weave, and sprint through a grid of beams without breaking the pattern. Sounds simple. It is not.

Beyond the maze, the rooms include mind-challenge formats like spot-the-difference and Charades, which shift the energy from physical to sharp and competitive. Every activity feeds into a points-based reward redemption system, so your performance across the full session actually means something when it's over.

Play hard. Score big. Win rewards. That's not just a tagline.

### 3. Claw Machines and the Arcade Lounge

Not every moment needs to be full intensity. The claw machine arcade lounge gives you somewhere to decompress between sessions, let younger kids play at their own pace, or just hang while the rest of your group finishes a round. Infusion Play is the only venue in Downtown Victoria with claw machines — which makes it a genuinely unique stop for families and casual visitors who want to ease into the fun.

## The Challenge + Immersion Bundle

Want to do it all in one visit? Book the Challenge + Immersion package. It combines the VR Arena and the Interactive Play rooms into a single session, typically running one to two hours depending on your group.

For couples, it's a two-hour shared story with light competition built in. For friend groups, it's the kind of night you'll actually talk about afterward. For corporate teams, it's a complete event without needing to wrangle multiple venues.

## Food and Drinks On-Site

After sprinting through a laser maze and surviving a zombie apocalypse, you'll want something good. Infusion Play makes handmade bubble tea, ice cream, and Bingsu on-site. These aren't vending machine snacks — they're actual food and drinks worth staying for.

For corporate groups especially, having food and entertainment in one place removes the biggest logistical headache of planning a team event.

## Who This Works Best For

Corporate groups: The venue handles large groups well, including events of 30 or more. Team competition across the challenge rooms and VR Arena creates natural moments for collaboration and friendly rivalry, and the on-site food and beverage offering means you have a complete event without leaving the building.

Families with kids aged 8 to 14: The laser maze and VR Arena hit the sweet spot for this age group. For children under 8, the sister location Infusion XR Arcade is the better fit — it runs room-scale VR titles including Elven Assassin archery and Mini Golf at a pace that works for smaller guests.

Couples: The Challenge + Immersion bundle was practically built for a date night. You're doing something together, competing a little, and finishing with food. That beats a movie every time.

Tourists and visitors: You're already downtown. The venue is walkable, walk-ins are welcome, and there's nothing to plan ahead. It's one of the most active things you can do in Victoria without leaving the city center.

